VM Provisioning Scripts
=======================

This directory contains scripts for creating and managing QEMU/KVM virtual
machines used in the Phantom Tracker project. VMs are provisioned from Debian
cloud images using cloud-init and run with KVM acceleration on x86_64 hosts.


Quick start
-----------

    bash create_vm.sh --name=foo --type=target --ssh-pubkey=~/.ssh/id_xxxx.pub

On first run, this downloads the Debian 12 base image (~400 MB), creates a
per-VM qcow2 overlay disk, generates a cloud-init seed ISO, and boots the VM.
Subsequent runs with the same --name skip provisioning and boot straight from
the existing disk.

For --type=target, cloud-init also installs the guest build and BPF packages:
make, gcc, gfortran, bpftool, gcc-bpf, libbpf-dev, pahole, linux-source-6.1,
libelf-dev, and zlib1g-dev. Noise VMs receive openssh-server, plus stress-ng
when launched with --mode=dev.

Target guests then install linux-headers-$(uname -r), resolving the kernel
version inside the guest. This is a separate, best-effort installation: an
unavailable header package produces a warning in /var/log/cloud-init-output.log
without preventing the ordinary development tools from being installed.
Guest module builds still require headers matching the running kernel.

These packages are installed during first-boot provisioning only. Existing
VM disks and seed ISOs are reused; install the packages manually in an
existing guest or re-provision it as shown below. For a guest affected by the
previous unavailable-header failure, repair the tools independently:

    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install -y openssh-server make gcc gfortran \
        bpftool gcc-bpf libbpf-dev pahole linux-source-6.1 libelf-dev zlib1g-dev
    sudo systemctl enable --now ssh

Then request headers separately with sudo apt-get install "linux-headers-$(uname -r)".
If they are no longer available, install a matching kernel/header pair and
boot that kernel before building guest modules.

Once the VM has booted (first boot takes 1-2 minutes for cloud-init):

    ssh -p 2222 debian@localhost

The default console password is "debian". To shut down cleanly from inside
the VM:

    sudo systemctl poweroff

To stop a running VM from the host:

    systemctl stop qemu-foo.scope          # graceful (ACPI shutdown)
    systemctl kill --kill-whom=all --signal=SIGKILL qemu-foo.scope  # immediate


create_vm.sh — arguments
------------------------

Required:
  --name=<name>         Name of the VM. Used for disk/ISO filenames and the
                        systemd scope. Allowed chars: letters, digits, . _ -
  --type=<type>         VM type. Must be either target or noise.
  --ssh-pubkey=<path>   Path to an SSH public key file to inject into the VM.

Optional:
  --mode=<user|dev>     VM mode (default: user). Host directory sharing is
                        enabled only in dev mode for development and testing.
  --password=<pass>     Password for the debian user (default: debian).
  --sockets=<n>         Number of CPU sockets (default: 1).
  --cores=<n>           Number of cores per socket (default: 2).
  --threads=<n>         Number of threads per core (default: 1).
                        Total vCPUs = sockets * cores * threads.
  --mem=<size>          RAM size, e.g. 4G or 512M (default: 4G).
  --disk-size=<size>    Disk image size, e.g. 20G (default: 20G).
  --base-image=<path>   Path to base qcow2 cloud image; downloaded from
                        Debian if absent (default: debian-12-generic-amd64.qcow2).
  --ssh-port=<port>     Host port forwarded to guest SSH (default: auto-select free port starting at 2222).
  --add-vsock=<bool>    Add a vhost-vsock device (default: false).
  --vsock-cid=<cid>     Guest CID for vhost-vsock. Required when
                        --add-vsock=true.
  --per-vm-cgroups=<bool>
                        Place the VM in a systemd transient scope under
                        machine.slice, matching libvirt's cgroup layout
                        (default: true). Requires systemd-run.
  --pin-to-socket=<bool>
                        Pin the VM to a specific NUMA node using numactl
                        (default: false).
  --socket-nr=<n>       NUMA node number to pin to. Required when
                        --pin-to-socket=true.

  --shared-dir=<path>  Host directory to create if absent and share read-only
                        via 9p in dev mode (default: disabled). Ignored in user
                        mode. Guest mount tag: hostshare.
  --shared-dir-readonly=<bool>
                        Enforce read-only access in QEMU (default: true).

Pass --help for a summary.


Examples
--------

Minimal VM, 2 vCPUs, 4G RAM:

    bash create_vm.sh \
        --name=foo \
        --type=target \
        --ssh-pubkey=~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

36-core VM pinned to NUMA node 0 with vsock:

    bash create_vm.sh \
        --name=bench \
        --type=target \
        --ssh-pubkey=~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub \
        --sockets=1 --cores=36 --threads=1 \
        --mem=45G \
        --pin-to-socket=true --socket-nr=0 \
        --add-vsock=true --vsock-cid=4

Development VM with a shared host directory (new or existing disks):

    bash create_vm.sh \
        --name=foo \
        --type=target \
        --ssh-pubkey=~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub \
        --mode=dev --shared-dir=./shared

The shared directory supports development and testing of Phantom Tracker,
including making host sources and build artifacts available in the guest.
The default user mode does not attach a shared directory, even if
--shared-dir is supplied. In dev mode, the script creates ./shared on the
host if needed. For target VMs, it waits for SSH and mounts the share at
/mnt/hostshare automatically, including when reusing an existing guest disk.
The mount uses ro by default, or rw with --shared-dir-readonly=false.

Automatic mounting is best effort. It requires --ssh-pubkey to end in .pub
and an unencrypted matching private key alongside it. Only that identity is
used, with no fallback to other keys, agents, or passwords. The guest must
authorize that key for debian and permit passwordless sudo (configured by
cloud-init). Host-key trust must already be established manually, for example
by connecting with ssh target once; no host keys are accepted automatically.
Missing/mismatched keys, authentication failures, and mount errors produce
a warning without stopping the VM. Only connection readiness errors are
retried. Existing mounts are left untouched.

Noise VMs require manual mounting. These commands also provide a fallback
if automatic mounting fails:

    sudo mkdir -p /mnt/hostshare
    sudo mount -t 9p -o trans=virtio,version=9p2000.L,ro hostshare /mnt/hostshare

After the first successful automatic mount, the guest's /etc/fstab is backed
up to /etc/fstab.phantom-tracker.bak and an entry is appended if neither the
share nor its mount point already has one. Existing entries are preserved.
For the default read-only export, the generated entry is:

    hostshare /mnt/hostshare 9p trans=virtio,version=9p2000.L,ro,nofail,x-systemd.mount-timeout=15s 0 0

On subsequent guest boots, systemd attempts the mount once with a 15-second
timeout. Failure does not block boot, including when the VM is started
without a share in user mode. No SSH connection is needed for these later
attempts. If the boot-time mount failed, the launcher does not retry it in
the same boot. Diagnose it inside the guest with:

    journalctl -b -u mnt-hostshare.mount

If initial SSH authentication or mounting fails, no fstab entry is added.
When changing ro/rw for a previously configured guest, update its existing
fstab entry manually; automatic setup leaves that entry untouched.

Start the VM with --mode=dev and the same --shared-dir each time. QEMU enforces
read-only access, even if the guest tries to remount read-write. To allow writes, 
use --shared-dir-readonly=false and mount with rw instead of ro. Host access
uses the account running QEMU. Omitting --shared-dir disables sharing.

Sharing uses security_model=none to expose ordinary host files and symlinks,
including build trees copied into the share on the host. Keep copied symlink 
targets within the shared tree, using relative links where possible. Absolute
host paths resolve in the guest's filesystem. In case the need of changing the
sharing mode, shut down and relaunch QEMU, then remount the share. A guest-only
reboot does not change the QEMU export settings.

Re-provision a VM (delete its disk and seed ISO first):

    rm foo.qcow2 foo-seed.iso
    bash create_vm.sh --name=foo --type=target --ssh-pubkey=~/.ssh/id_xxxx.pub


Library files (lib/)
--------------------

  args.sh        Named argument parsing. Provides declare_arg, parse_args,
                 and print_usage. Arguments use --key=value syntax.

  cloudinit.sh   cloud-init provisioning for Debian VMs. Downloads the base
                 image, creates a per-VM qcow2 overlay, and generates a
                 seed ISO using xorriso.

  distro.sh      Host distro detection and install-hint helpers. Used by
                 check_cmd to print the correct package manager command when
                 a dependency is missing.

  numa.sh        NUMA topology helpers reading /sys/devices/system/node.
                 Provides numa_check, numa_node_count, and numa_cpulist.

  CONTRIBUTING-IDEAS
                 Suggested improvements for new contributors. Read this if
                 you want to contribute but are not sure where to start.


Dependencies
------------

Runtime (checked at startup):
  qemu-system-x86_64    Debian/Ubuntu: qemu-system-x86
                        Fedora/RHEL:   qemu-kvm
  numactl               All distros:   numactl
  udevadm               For target VM host-device permissions (Debian: udev).

Target VM users must belong to the host's kvm group. Host module setup installs
a udev rule granting that group read/write access to /dev/host_ivshmem and
waits for udev before launching QEMU. After adding group membership, log out
and log back in for it to take effect.

First-boot provisioning only:
  wget                  For downloading the base cloud image.
  xorriso               For generating the cloud-init seed ISO.
                        Debian/Ubuntu: xorriso
                        Arch:          libisoburn
                        Gentoo:        libisoburn

Optional:
  systemd-run           For per-VM cgroups (part of systemd, present on most
                        distros).
  9p sharing            For --shared-dir: QEMU must support 9p, and the guest
                        kernel must support 9p and 9pnet_virtio.
